    "reading": {
        "body": "Seven AI developer relations and education roles were open on 8 October 2026, and not one of them is a research or infrastructure job. The hiring is for people who teach, write, and build community around AI systems that already exist.\n\nThe pattern is that the demand sits at the adoption layer. Databricks is hiring two senior developer advocates for AI and machine learning, one in Seattle and one in San Francisco. SambaNova wants a senior developer advocate for AI content and community in San Jose. Lambda wants developer relations for enterprise AI. DirectedLINK is filling two AI instructor roles in Los Angeles County, and the U.S. Air Force Academy is hiring a professor of STEM and artificial intelligence. These are the roles that translate a model or a platform into something a working developer or student can actually use.\n\nFor AI specifically, this is a second-order signal. The compute buildout is upstream and invisible here. What these postings show is the layer above it, where vendors compete for the attention of the people who will write the code, and where institutions like the Air Force Academy are trying to grow that workforce from the classroom up. Salaries appear on only three of the seven postings, ranging from $85,423 to $168,838 for the federal professorship and $92,323 to $107,282 for the two instructor roles, so pay is mostly unstated.\n\nWhat this does not tell you is volume or trend. Seven postings is a snapshot of one function on one day, drawn from aggregators and company boards, and it says nothing about whether this category is growing or shrinking. It also says nothing about the compute, land, or power constraints that shape what these advocates will be promoting.",
